extreme temperatures very little rain  and intense light plants in the Negev  desert are subject to serious  environmental stress how they cope with  it is one of the main topics of research  here at Israel's blaustein Institute in  sediba care the researchers are also  investigating how desert plants can be  used in agriculture because they're able  to cope with the harsh environmental  conditions  in this experiment the plants get to  enjoy a ride on a carousel each  container can simulate a different  environmental condition  Naftali lazarevich is an expert on  irrigation he and his colleagues  developed this setup right now he's  using it to study bell pepper plants we  want to understand the process of water  flow from the soil into the plant under  certain conditions after we understand  the process we can optimize it and for  instance adjust the irrigation frequency  here at the blaustein institute the  focus is on basic research but  lazarevich knows that it isn't easy to  communicate the results of his work to  farmers that's why he sometimes makes  the hour-and-a-half drive to the Arava  research and development center near the  Dead Sea  from grapes to peppers to millet the  researchers here are investigating  whether plants behave the same way in  real-life conditions as they do in the  laboratory the goal is to produce  research that will directly benefit  farmers today he wants to see how the  date trees are faring but the research  here doesn't only focus on dates grapes  and millet they're also looking at fish  PhD student Tom gloom beds explains that  fish gained biomass quite rapidly and  their excrement can be used as plant  fertilizer any water that's available  can be used and recycled now it's being  cleaned in these barrels  it will then flow next door to the  greenhouse where it's used to water the  lettuce there there's a pipe running  through there that gives oxygen to the  plant roots and the plants grow in the  water take up the nitrate and then the  water without the nitrates and other  nutrients that the plant of course takes  up flows back to the fish so they get  the clean water back again  rice is normally cultivated in areas  that have plenty of water but here the  researchers are testing a drip  irrigation system I'm not saying that we  will grow wise in Israel in the future  but here in this area we have many  volunteers that coming from other  nations and they work here and part of  them are from East Asia and they study  drip irrigation and they will studied it  will we discover with their eyes that  they are familiar with when these  students return home they'll bring what  they learned with them rice can be grown  using much less water also dramatically  cutting nitrogen emissions that damage  the environment
 

